4. Online

This page must respect the architecture we already established:

NET, ACCOUNT and HISCORES are independent states.

Never derive network connectivity from authentication state or highscore synchronization state.

A user may:

have network access and no account
have an account but disable online highscores
have a cached authenticated identity while currently offline
have no account at all and use Bootcade permanently as a completely local launcher

An account is optional. Online features are optional. Bootcade must remain fully usable without either.

When signed out, show a proper Sign In presentation matching the new visual language. Do not imply that the user is in some incomplete or erroneous state.

When signed in, the Account/Profile area may display real information available from the Bootcade account/profile APIs:

username
avatar
country if available
account/member information if available
relevant statistics if the backend actually exposes them

Do not fabricate profile data merely to reproduce the mockup.

Likewise, only expose Online Feature toggles corresponding to real functionality. The mockup establishes the visual direction; it does not authorize silently implementing fake sync/community/telemetry features.

Network Status must use the independent asynchronous connectivity probe already implemented.

5. Emulator

This page must be designed from the beginning so Bootcade is not permanently tied to FBNeo.

Today FinalBurn Neo is the configured emulator, but the UI architecture must support additional emulators later without requiring another Settings redesign.

Use the mockup's two-panel structure:

Left: emulator registry/list

Each emulator entry can expose:

emulator logo
name
configured/not configured status
active/enabled status where applicable

Do not hard-code the entire page around FinalBurn Neo widgets.

Right: selected emulator configuration

For FBNeo, display real information when detectable:

FinalBurn Neo logo
name
detected version
executable path
executable status

Provide the relevant existing actions such as:

Browse
Download
Test Emulator

Emulator-specific options belong in the selected emulator's configuration panel rather than becoming global Bootcade settings.

The implementation should make adding another emulator later primarily a matter of registering its metadata/configuration capabilities rather than rebuilding this page.

Again: entries such as MAME/RetroArch/Flycast/etc. in the mockup illustrate the extensible design. Do not pretend they are currently supported if they are not.

6. Visual implementation requirements

This is important.

I do not want the current GNOME-looking Settings dialog with a few CSS changes.

I want the visual language shown in the four mockups:

dark Bootcade panels
subtle borders
rounded containers
purple active/accent states
clear information hierarchy
generous but controlled spacing
custom SVG icons where required
compact descriptive secondary text
polished switches
polished buttons
consistent status colors

Do not substitute stock GNOME icons when they visibly break the mockup. Create the required SVGs.

At the same time, avoid turning the application into a pile of one-off drawing code. Establish reusable Bootcade Settings components/styles.

7. Functional safety

This redesign must not break working behavior.

Before changing each page, inventory its current controls and callbacks.

Existing functionality must either:

remain available in the redesigned page, or
be explicitly reported to me before being removed.

In particular, do not touch unrelated game startup, ROM scanning, emulator launching, highscore or authentication logic merely to achieve the visual redesign.

Separate presentation refactoring from behavior changes.

